        "en" to GemDetails(
            "Fossil bone, or agatised dinosaur bone, is ancient bone whose pores and canals were filled, and then its material replaced, by silica or other minerals. After the animal's death, the bone, quickly buried in river sediments, was permeated by groundwater carrying silica, iron and manganese; chalcedony, agate or jasper precipitated in the cells of the spongy bone, preserving its characteristic honeycomb cellular structure. The colours, red, yellow, blue, black or grey, come from metal oxides in the water.",
            "Pseudomorph: bone structure (cells, Haversian canals) filled with microcrystalline chalcedony and quartz (trigonal), sometimes with calcite, baryte or uranium minerals. No cleavage; uneven to conchoidal fracture. Hardness 6.5 to 7 for well-silicified specimens, lower if mineralisation is incomplete; specific gravity 2.5 to 2.7; vitreous to waxy lustre when polished; opaque. Only well-agatised bone with coloured cells is cut into cabochons; the rest remain specimens. Some bone from the region can be slightly radioactive (uranium): keep it in a display case, not in constant contact with skin.",
